    "description": "The Aras is a transboundary river connecting eastern Türkiye, the southern Caucasus, and northwestern Iran before joining the Kura. Its Iranian reaches combine an international boundary, agricultural water resources, distinctive geological landscapes, and historic crossings. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Aras River", "Northwestern Iran", "Transboundary waters", "Caucasus", "River landscapes"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T14:11:59Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T14:11:59Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Aras River: Iran’s Northwestern Border and Shared Waters",
      "description": "Explore the Aras River’s international geography, historic crossings, geological setting, and the evidence needed to assess its water resources."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "course-and-connections",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A river connecting several countries",
        "content": "The Aras, also written Araz or Araks, belongs to a shared river system rather than an exclusively Iranian watershed. A basin study describes its origin in eastern Türkiye and its passage along international boundaries before it joins the Kura in Azerbaijan. The combined waters ultimately reach the Caspian Sea. For readers approaching Iran from its interior plateau, this is an important geographical distinction: the Aras connects the country’s northwest to the southern Caucasus and the Caspian drainage system. [s1]\n\nThe same study distinguishes the Iranian portion of the watershed from the international basin as a whole, placing it across West Azerbaijan, East Azerbaijan, and Ardabil provinces. A watershed is the land draining toward a river; it is broader than the river channel and includes tributaries, agricultural land, and settlements. Consequently, a statistic about the Iranian watershed cannot automatically describe conditions across the entire Aras system. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "geological-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the landscape beside the water",
        "content": "UNESCO describes the Aras River as the northern boundary of Aras UNESCO Global Geopark, separating its Iranian territory from Armenia and Azerbaijan. The geopark offers a useful introduction to the surrounding landscape, although its boundaries should not be confused with those of the river basin. Its cliffs and highlands expose carbonate rocks, while other areas contain sandstone, shale, marl, and igneous formations. [s2]\n\nAt the Alli-Bashi geosite, fossil-bearing strata preserve a succession across the Permian–Triassic boundary, a major division in geological time. Nearby badlands show a different process: erosion cutting closely spaced channels into relatively soft material. These features make the valley more than a line on a political map. They reveal a landscape whose rocks and landforms can be studied alongside its human history. UNESCO also identifies wildlife conservation as an important dimension of the geopark. This designation documents the surrounding territory’s geological and conservation significance; it does not establish the water quality of the river itself. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historic-crossings",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A boundary that was also a crossing",
        "content": "The Khudafarin, or Khoda Afarin, bridges illustrate the Aras’s role as a connection between communities. Azerbaijan’s submission to UNESCO’s Tentative List locates them between Jabrayil and Iran’s Khoda Afarin County. It describes bridge piers supported by natural rocks in the riverbed, showing how builders used the gorge’s physical structure to establish a crossing. [s3]\n\nThe submission associates the bridges with caravan traffic, migration, and military movement. It also records competing accounts of their construction and rebuilding, so a single precise founding date would conceal uncertainty. The record is a national heritage submission hosted by UNESCO, rather than an independent resolution of every historical claim or an inscription on the World Heritage List. Taken together, the river’s course, geological setting, and bridges offer complementary ways to understand the Aras: as shared water, a landscape, and a corridor of movement. Assessing its present allocation and environmental pressures requires a separate, current body of hydrological evidence. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
