    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "The Karkheh links the Zagros Mountains with the lowlands of Khuzestan and the Hawizeh–Azim marsh system along the Iran–Iraq border. Its basin illustrates how farming, river regulation, floodplain habitats, and downstream water availability are connected. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Karkheh River", "Zagros Mountains", "Khuzestan", "Wetlands", "Water management", "Agriculture"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T14:11:59Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T14:11:59Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Karkheh River: From the Zagros to Khuzestan’s Marshes",
      "description": "Understand the Karkheh’s mountain-to-marsh geography, agricultural importance, riverine habitats, and documented pressures on downstream flows."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "mountains-to-marshes",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From mountain tributaries to a border wetland",
        "content": "The Karkheh is best understood as a chain of connections between uplands and lowlands. Its tributaries drain the Zagros Mountains of western Iran, while its lower course crosses Khuzestan toward the marshes along the Iraqi border. Encyclopaedia Iranica explains that the name Karkheh applies to the river below the meeting of the Seymareh and Kashkan. Following only the named lower river therefore misses much of the system supplying it. [s1]\n\nThe downstream destination is the wetland system known by variants including Hawizeh and Hur al-Azim. The Karkheh does not simply run directly into the open Persian Gulf. Its lower waters enter a landscape of marshes and channels associated with the Mesopotamian lowlands. Iranica emphasizes the contrast between mountain valleys and the hot, saline plains downstream: irrigation, evaporation, drainage, and seasonal flooding help shape the river’s ecological setting. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "agriculture-and-water",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Agriculture links the upper and lower basin",
        "content": "Agricultural research in the basin has examined both rainfed farming, which depends principally on precipitation, and irrigated production. The published summary of an ICARDA partnership with Iranian agricultural researchers describes on-farm trials, investigation of upper–lower basin interactions, and assessment of water policies and institutions. These are linked questions: a useful farming intervention must be assessed in relation to the wider water system as well as the individual field. [s4]\n\nThe International Water Management Institute’s basin project similarly placed livelihoods alongside hydrology. Its research programme proposed water accounting, analysis of agricultural performance, and assessment of how alternative interventions might affect poverty and basin sustainability. These project descriptions establish research objectives and approaches; they do not demonstrate that every proposed intervention was adopted or produced lasting improvements. They nevertheless explain why the Karkheh cannot be understood through engineering infrastructure alone. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "downstream-change",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What declining flow evidence establishes",
        "content": "A study published in 2025 analysed precipitation and river-flow records extending through 2016. It found pronounced flow declines after the late 1990s, while most precipitation stations showed no statistically significant trend. The authors interpret the mismatch as evidence of the importance of upstream development, withdrawals, and fragmented management. This is a historical research finding, rather than a measurement of conditions on the editorial cutoff date. [s2]\n\nThe study connects reduced inflows to difficulties meeting the Karkheh reservoir’s purposes and to downstream marsh degradation. Its practical implication is that storage capacity alone cannot establish how much water remains available to users and ecosystems. The findings support examining the basin as a whole, while their observational design does not justify assigning every local shortage to one cause. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "riverine-habitats",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Habitats beyond the main reservoir",
        "content": "The Key Biodiversity Areas account describes lower-river woodland dominated by tamarisk and Euphrates poplar, with adjacent marshy depressions and seasonally flooded creeks. It records the importance of the river and floodplain for birds and identifies grazing, fuelwood cutting, farming, and hunting among historical pressures. Because the account’s assessment dates to 1994, its species and habitat descriptions should be read as a documented baseline, not a current census. [s3]\n\nTogether, these sources show why the river’s value extends beyond the water passing a dam: its tributaries support agriculture, its banks sustain woodland, and its lower flows help maintain marsh habitat. A sound account of the Karkheh keeps those connected settings visible while distinguishing historical evidence from present monitoring. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "hydrological-connections",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "The Karkheh’s mountain-to-marsh connection",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Zagros tributary catchments\"] -->|\"Drain toward\"| B[\"Seymareh River\"]\n  C[\"Kashkan catchment\"] -->|\"Drains toward\"| D[\"Kashkan River\"]\n  B -->|\"Joins Kashkan to form\"| E[\"Karkheh River\"]\n  D -->|\"Joins Seymareh to form\"| E\n  E -->|\"Passes through regulation at\"| F[\"Karkheh Dam and reservoir\"]\n  F -->|\"Releases water to\"| G[\"Lower Karkheh\"]\n  G -->|\"Contributes inflow to\"| H[\"Hawizeh-Azim marsh system\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows describe drainage, confluence, regulation, and downstream contribution. This simplified diagram omits individual irrigation diversions and other tributaries; it does not quantify flows or imply that the marsh receives water only from the Karkheh.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
